{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"**Verified SCOPe facts (stable release 2.08).** Ferritin (a.25.1.1, sunid 47241), Ribonucleotide reductase-like (a.25.1.2, sunid 47253) and Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (a.25.1.3, sunid 100951) are three separate families. All three have the same parent, the superfamily Ferritin-like (a.25.1, sunid 47240). That superfamily sits in the fold Ferritin-like (a.25, sunid 47239) in class a, All alpha proteins (sunid 46456). Other families in the same superfamily include YciF-like (a.25.1.4), half-ferritin (a.25.1.5), MiaE-like (a.25.1.7) and others, so the superfamily covers varied chemistries. The superfamily-level annotation in 2.08 reads 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. The homology records for all three families and the superfamily show homology implied at their own level, with no warnings or heterogeneity flags. The Mn catalase family is annotated as automatically mapped to Pfam PF05067.

**Interpretation.** Three different families under one superfamily means SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry at the superfamily level. The families are separate because their sequence and functional similarity is closer within each family than between them. So ferritins (iron storage and ferroxidase), RNR-like proteins (the R2/diiron-type subunits and related diiron proteins) and Mn catalases (peroxide disproportionation by a dimanganese centre) are best read as a divergent, probably homologous four-helix-bundle superfamily. Evolution kept a core bundle that holds a dinuclear metal site and changed the metal preference, ligand environment, oligomeric assembly and chemistry. This is the usual picture of a shared scaffold being adapted to different reactions. It is not evidence that the proteins are the same enzyme or that the chemistry is conserved.

**Limits.** (1) Shared superfamily placement supports common ancestry but does not support transferring function, substrate, metal identity, mechanism or oligomeric state. A ferritin cannot be assumed to have catalase or RNR activity, or the reverse. Using one family's structure as a modeling template for another's active-site chemistry would be an unsafe transfer. (2) The family level is the closer SCOPe relationship, and the superfamily link is remote. The three families should not be merged or treated as interchangeable. (3) The fold and class levels (a.25, class a) only indicate structural similarity. Ancestry is asserted in SCOPe only from the superfamily level up to the shared homology call, and I did not inspect alignments, structures or literature here. The API responses do not show the curators' sequence, structural or literature rationale for grouping these families. (4) I did not retrieve domain-level lineages for particular PDB entries, quality scores or release history, so I make no claim about specific representative structures or past reclassifications. (5) Had the families been in different superfamilies, that would only mean SCOPe makes no positive homology assertion, not that they are analogs or convergent. That is not the case here.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47239/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47241/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47253/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/parents","/